1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Welcome to Server Administration 1.

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:03,850
This is Topic 1.

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:05,700
1, called Server Hardware.

4
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:11,150
Before you install Windows Server, it
helps to know what it runs on.

5
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:18,200
In this video you will look at how servers
are built, how they are mounted in racks,

6
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:24,050
how they get power and cooling, and how
admins manage them from far away.

7
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:29,100
You will also check what Windows Server
2025 needs from the hardware.

8
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:34,150
It all applies to any brand of server you
meet at work.

9
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:40,000
Here are the six things you will be able
to do by the end.

10
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:44,650
First, explain what makes a server
different from a desktop PC.

11
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:48,500
Second, read a rack diagram and count rack
units.

12
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:51,950
Third, name the main parts inside a
server.

13
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:56,200
Fourth, plan power and cooling, including
a simple UPS estimate.

14
00:00:56,450 --> 00:01:00,850
Fifth, tell in-band from out-of-band
management, and explain UEFI, Secure Boot

15
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:02,300
and the TPM.

16
00:01:02,550 --> 00:01:07,750
And sixth, choose hardware for a workload
and check the Windows Server 2025

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,200
minimums.

18
00:01:09,450 --> 00:01:12,650
A server is not just a fast PC.

19
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:16,500
A desktop runs during work hours for one
person.

20
00:01:16,750 --> 00:01:22,750
A server runs all day, every day, for
years, and many users depend on it.

21
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,600
That changes the parts.

22
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:30,450
Servers use ECC memory, which finds and
fixes memory errors before they crash a

23
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:31,900
service.

24
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:37,750
They have spares built in: two power
supplies, drives you can swap while the

25
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:39,600
server runs, and RAID.

26
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:44,250
And you can manage them remotely, even
when Windows is down.

27
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:47,700
Remember three words: reliability,
redundancy and remote management.

28
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:51,550
Servers come in three main shapes, called
form factors.

29
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,800
A tower stands on the floor like a large
PC.

30
00:01:56,050 --> 00:01:58,050
It suits a small office.

31
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:02,300
A rack server bolts into a 19-inch rack on
rails.

32
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:08,550
Its height is measured in rack units, and
many servers share one rack, its cables

33
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:10,000
and its power.

34
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:14,650
A blade is a thin server that slides into
a chassis.

35
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:20,100
The chassis shares power, cooling and
network links with every blade, so blades

36
00:02:20,350 --> 00:02:21,550
are very dense.

37
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:24,200
You find them at large sites.

38
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:26,450
Let's read a rack diagram.

39
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:31,500
Each numbered space is one rack unit, or
1U, which is 1.

40
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:32,950
75 inches high.

41
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:37,600
You count from the bottom, so U1 is the
lowest space.

42
00:02:37,850 --> 00:02:42,250
A 2U server takes two spaces, a 4U server
takes four.

43
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:44,500
Full-height racks are often 42U.

44
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:52,350
Now count this rack with me: 1, plus 1,
plus 2, plus 1, plus 4, plus 2 is 11U

45
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:53,800
used.

46
00:02:54,050 --> 00:03:00,450
Only U10 is free, so a new 2U server will
not fit anywhere in this rack.

47
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:05,900
A socket holds one CPU, and servers often
have one or two sockets.

48
00:03:06,150 --> 00:03:12,150
Each CPU has several cores, and each core
can usually run two threads at once.

49
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:17,600
Multiply them: two sockets times eight
cores times two threads gives 32 logical

50
00:03:17,850 --> 00:03:19,050
processors.

51
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:24,500
Memory in a server is ECC, usually
registered DIMMs of the current generation

52
00:03:24,750 --> 00:03:25,950
such as DDR5.

53
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:31,000
Network adapters, or NICs, run at 1, 10 or
25 gigabit Ethernet.

54
00:03:31,250 --> 00:03:36,450
Teaming joins ports, so traffic keeps
flowing if one cable or switch fails.

55
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:39,500
Drives connect through one of three
interfaces.

56
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:45,750
SATA is the low-cost choice for big
drives, at up to 6 gigabits per second.

57
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
SAS is the enterprise choice.

58
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:54,650
It runs at 12 gigabits and has two ports,
for a spare path to each drive.

59
00:03:54,900 --> 00:04:00,100
NVMe drives are solid-state and connect
straight to PCI Express lanes, for the

60
00:04:00,350 --> 00:04:01,550
lowest delay.

61
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:06,200
Hot-swap bays let you replace a drive
while the server runs.

62
00:04:06,450 --> 00:04:10,850
A RAID controller joins drives so one
failure loses no data.

63
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:13,500
RAID levels come in Topic 8.

64
00:04:13,750 --> 00:04:14,950
1.

65
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:20,800
Each server has two hot-swappable power
supplies, or PSUs, and either one can run

66
00:04:21,050 --> 00:04:22,250
the whole server.

67
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:28,900
Follow the diagram: PSU 1 is fed by
circuit A through UPS A and PDU A.

68
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:31,550
PSU 2 uses the B path.

69
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:36,200
If one circuit, UPS or PDU fails, the
server keeps running.

70
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:41,250
The UPS is a battery that takes over in a
power cut.

71
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:43,900
It gives you minutes, not hours.

72
00:04:44,150 --> 00:04:49,750
The PDU is the rack's power strip, and
some PDUs can measure the load.

73
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,800
How big a UPS do you need?

74
00:04:53,050 --> 00:04:54,250
Follow four steps.

75
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:58,100
One, add the watts of everything you plug
in.

76
00:04:58,350 --> 00:05:00,350
Here that is 600 watts.

77
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:04,200
Two, add 25 percent headroom, which gives
750 watts.

78
00:05:04,450 --> 00:05:08,450
Three, convert to VA by dividing by the
power factor.

79
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:10,300
This course uses 0.

80
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:13,350
9, so you get about 834 VA.

81
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:19,600
Four, pick a UPS where both ratings are
higher, like 1000 VA and 900 watts.

82
00:05:19,850 --> 00:05:21,050
Why both?

83
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:27,300
Watts are the real work, and many units
have a lower watt rating than VA.

84
00:05:27,550 --> 00:05:29,550
Then check the runtime chart.

85
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:32,600
Servers breathe air from front to back.

86
00:05:32,850 --> 00:05:38,850
Fans pull cool air in at the front and
push hot air out the back.

87
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:45,900
In a server room, rows of racks face each
other across a cold aisle, and stand back

88
00:05:46,150 --> 00:05:48,550
to back across a hot aisle.

89
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:52,000
That way no server breathes another
server's exhaust.

90
00:05:52,250 --> 00:05:55,450
Remember the blanking panels from the rack
slide?

91
00:05:55,700 --> 00:06:01,700
Without them, hot air loops through the
empty spaces back to the front, and the

92
00:06:01,950 --> 00:06:03,550
servers slowly heat up.

93
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:07,000
Windows has crashed and you are at home.

94
00:06:07,250 --> 00:06:08,850
You need out-of-band management.

95
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:12,700
Most servers have a BMC, a baseboard
management controller.

96
00:06:12,950 --> 00:06:18,150
It is a small computer with its own
network port, on standby power.

97
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:24,800
It works even when the server is off, as
long as the power cord is in.

98
00:06:25,050 --> 00:06:32,250
Through it you can power the server on or
off, see its screen, mount an ISO file as

99
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:33,700
a virtual DVD.

100
00:06:33,950 --> 00:06:36,750
Vendors call it iDRAC, iLO or XClarity.

101
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,200
The standards are IPMI and the newer
Redfish.

102
00:06:40,450 --> 00:06:44,050
Modern servers use UEFI, which replaced
the legacy BIOS.

103
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:49,900
UEFI boots from GPT disks, which can be
larger than 2 terabytes, and supports

104
00:06:50,150 --> 00:06:51,350
Secure Boot.

105
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:57,200
Secure Boot only runs boot loaders signed
by a trusted key, which blocks boot-time

106
00:06:57,450 --> 00:06:58,650
malware.

107
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:04,900
The TPM is a security chip that stores
keys and records each step of startup.

108
00:07:05,150 --> 00:07:10,750
For Windows Server 2025, Microsoft lists
UEFI with Secure Boot and a TPM as

109
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,200
required only for certain features, such
as BitLocker.

110
00:07:14,450 --> 00:07:18,850
If there is a hardware TPM, it must be
version 2.

111
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:20,300
0.

112
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:23,350
Here are the minimums from Microsoft
Learn.

113
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:26,000
The CPU must be a 1.

114
00:07:26,250 --> 00:07:29,850
4 gigahertz 64-bit processor with a few
required features.

115
00:07:30,100 --> 00:07:35,700
Memory is 2 gigabytes for Server Core and
2 for Desktop Experience, with 4

116
00:07:35,950 --> 00:07:37,150
recommended.

117
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:40,200
Disk is 32 gigabytes, an absolute minimum.

118
00:07:40,450 --> 00:07:44,850
The network adapter must reach at least
one gigabit per second.

119
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:46,300
Two tips.

120
00:07:46,550 --> 00:07:50,550
A minimum is not a recommendation: size
for your roles.

121
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:57,600
And setup can fail in a VM with 1,024
megabytes, so give it 1,280 or more during

122
00:07:57,850 --> 00:07:59,050
setup.

123
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:02,100
Start with the workload, not the catalog.

124
00:08:02,350 --> 00:08:08,750
A small office file server can be a tower
with one socket, moderate memory, and big

125
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:10,200
drives in RAID.

126
00:08:10,450 --> 00:08:16,450
A virtualization host carries many virtual
machines, so it gets two sockets, a lot of

127
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:22,700
memory, fast SSDs, teamed 10 or 25 gigabit
ports, and two power supplies on separate

128
00:08:22,950 --> 00:08:24,150
feeds.

129
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:31,200
A branch domain controller is small, but
nobody at the branch can fix it, so a UPS

130
00:08:31,450 --> 00:08:33,450
and a BMC matter most.

131
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:36,900
Always ask: what happens if this server
stops?

132
00:08:37,150 --> 00:08:40,350
Windows can show you the hardware it sees.

133
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:45,000
Run msinfo32 to open System Information,
and stay on System Summary.

134
00:08:45,250 --> 00:08:46,850
Look for four rows.

135
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:52,700
Processor shows the CPU name and how many
cores and logical processors Windows sees.

136
00:08:52,950 --> 00:08:55,350
BIOS Mode says UEFI or Legacy.

137
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:58,800
Secure Boot State says On, Off or
Unsupported.

138
00:08:59,050 --> 00:09:01,850
And Installed Physical Memory shows the
RAM.

139
00:09:02,100 --> 00:09:08,100
In the pod your servers are virtual
machines, so these values come from the VM

140
00:09:08,350 --> 00:09:10,750
settings, not from a physical box.

141
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,400
Your values will differ from mine.

142
00:09:13,650 --> 00:09:16,850
You can read the same facts with
PowerShell.

143
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:21,500
Get-ComputerInfo with the Property
parameter picks just the values you want,

144
00:09:21,750 --> 00:09:24,150
and Format-List shows one per line.

145
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:30,000
Total physical memory is in bytes: about 4
gigabytes here, and Windows shows slightly

146
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:31,450
less.

147
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:36,900
Confirm-SecureBootUEFI returns True when
Secure Boot is on, False when it is off,

148
00:09:37,150 --> 00:09:40,350
and an error on a legacy BIOS system.

149
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:44,600
Get-Tpm tells you whether a TPM is present
and ready.

150
00:09:44,850 --> 00:09:47,250
Run the last two as administrator.

151
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:49,500
Again, your values will differ.

152
00:09:49,750 --> 00:09:53,350
Here are four mistakes people make with
server hardware.

153
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:57,200
First, plugging both power supplies into
the same PDU.

154
00:09:57,450 --> 00:09:59,850
Use the A and B feeds.

155
00:10:00,100 --> 00:10:03,700
Second, sizing a UPS by its VA number
only.

156
00:10:03,950 --> 00:10:07,150
Check the watt rating too, and leave
headroom.

157
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:12,200
Third, leaving the BMC on the user network
with its default password.

158
00:10:12,450 --> 00:10:18,850
Anyone who reaches it controls the server,
so give it its own network and a strong

159
00:10:19,100 --> 00:10:20,300
password.

160
00:10:20,550 --> 00:10:22,950
Fourth, leaving empty rack spaces open.

161
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:27,200
Fit blanking panels to keep hot air behind
the rack.

162
00:10:27,450 --> 00:10:29,050
Time to check yourself.

163
00:10:29,300 --> 00:10:33,700
They buy a 2U file server and a 4U storage
shelf.

164
00:10:33,950 --> 00:10:38,350
How much rack space is that, in U and in
inches?

165
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:44,200
A server froze at 2 in the morning and
does not answer Remote Desktop.

166
00:10:44,450 --> 00:10:47,250
How can you restart it from home?

167
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:53,500
The load is 600 watts, and a UPS is rated
1000 VA and 600 watts.

168
00:10:53,750 --> 00:10:55,750
Is it a good choice?

169
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,800
And they want BitLocker with a TPM.

170
00:10:59,050 --> 00:11:01,050
What does the hardware need?

171
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:02,500
Pause and answer.

172
00:11:02,750 --> 00:11:03,950
Let's check.

173
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:08,200
One: 2U plus 4U is 6U, and 6 times 1.

174
00:11:08,450 --> 00:11:10,050
75 inches is 10.

175
00:11:10,300 --> 00:11:12,300
5 inches, in one block.

176
00:11:12,550 --> 00:11:14,150
Two: use the BMC.

177
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:19,200
Its remote console and power controls work
even when Windows has stopped.

178
00:11:19,450 --> 00:11:20,650
Three: no.

179
00:11:20,900 --> 00:11:25,700
600 watts is the full watt rating, so
there is no headroom.

180
00:11:25,950 --> 00:11:29,550
Pick a UPS rated at 750 watts or more.

181
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:32,600
Four: the server needs a TPM 2.

182
00:11:32,850 --> 00:11:34,050
0 chip.

183
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:40,300
Microsoft lists a TPM as required only for
certain features, and BitLocker is one of

184
00:11:40,550 --> 00:11:41,750
them.

185
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,200
Let me show you SRV01 in the pod.

186
00:11:45,450 --> 00:11:47,850
I am signed in as Administrator.

187
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:52,100
I press Windows and R, type msinfo32, and
press Enter.

188
00:11:52,350 --> 00:11:54,750
System Information opens on System
Summary.

189
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,600
Here is the processor.

190
00:11:56,850 --> 00:12:02,050
This is a virtual machine, so these values
come from the VM settings.

191
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:06,300
Here is BIOS Mode, and below it Secure
Boot State.

192
00:12:06,550 --> 00:12:08,950
And here is Installed Physical Memory.

193
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:10,800
Now PowerShell, as administrator.

194
00:12:11,050 --> 00:12:15,050
Get-ComputerInfo piped to Format-List
shows the logical processors, the memory

195
00:12:15,300 --> 00:12:17,700
in bytes and the firmware type.

196
00:12:17,950 --> 00:12:20,350
Confirm-SecureBootUEFI and Get-Tpm finish
the picture.

197
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:21,800
Let's sum up.

198
00:12:22,050 --> 00:12:24,850
Servers add reliability, redundancy and
remote management.

199
00:12:25,100 --> 00:12:27,900
They come as towers, racks or blades.

200
00:12:28,150 --> 00:12:33,350
Inside you find ECC memory, SATA, SAS or
NVMe drives, and hot-swap bays.

201
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:40,400
Two power supplies go on two feeds, and
you size a UPS in both watts and VA.

202
00:12:40,650 --> 00:12:43,450
The BMC works when Windows does not.

203
00:12:43,700 --> 00:12:46,100
UEFI, Secure Boot and TPM 2.

204
00:12:46,350 --> 00:12:47,950
0 protect the startup.

205
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:49,400
Lab 1.

206
00:12:49,650 --> 00:12:52,850
1 is a scenario lab with no pod.

207
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:56,700
It takes about 35 minutes and is practice
only.

208
00:12:56,950 --> 00:12:58,950
See you in the lab.
